<cm:compliance-check-name>19.1.3.2 Ensure &apos;Password protect the screen saver&apos; is set to &apos;Enabled&apos;</cm:compliance-check-name>
<cm:compliance-check-id>2bc75088c1950361db048082d36be644f276a71f7be84f06b076ad6b4226655c</cm:compliance-check-id>
<cm:compliance-actual-value>&apos;Non-compliant items:
  HKU\S-1-5-21-2864493588-2528942772-2145745040-1000\Software\Policies\Microsoft\Windows\Control Panel\Desktop - &apos;</cm:compliance-actual-value>
<cm:compliance-source>custom</cm:compliance-source>
<cm:compliance-audit-file>CIS_MS_Windows_10_Enterprise_Level_1_v2.0.0.audit</cm:compliance-audit-file>
<cm:compliance-policy-value>&apos;1&apos;</cm:compliance-policy-value>
<cm:compliance-functional-id>055570a927</cm:compliance-functional-id>
<cm:compliance-info>This setting determines whether screen savers used on the computer are password protected.

The recommended state for this setting is: Enabled.

Rationale:

If a user forgets to lock their computer when they walk away, it is possible that a passerby will hijack it. Configuring a timed screen saver with password lock will help to protect against these hijacks.

Impact:

All screen savers are password protected. The &apos;Password protected&apos; checkbox on the Screen Saver dialog in the Personalization or Display Control Panel will be disabled, preventing users from changing the password protection setting.</cm:compliance-info>
<cm:compliance-result>FAILED</cm:compliance-result>
