<cm:compliance-check-name>9.3.5 Ensure &apos;Windows Firewall: Public: Settings: Apply local firewall rules&apos; is set to &apos;No&apos;</cm:compliance-check-name>
<cm:compliance-check-id>9a8b85dad4df8454bd52deb4b9ec890f8bebae34baa2eb05990354d5d1a79d88</cm:compliance-check-id>
<cm:compliance-actual-value>NULL</cm:compliance-actual-value>
<cm:compliance-source>custom</cm:compliance-source>
<cm:compliance-audit-file>CIS_MS_Windows_10_Enterprise_Level_1_v2.0.0.audit</cm:compliance-audit-file>
<cm:compliance-policy-value>0</cm:compliance-policy-value>
<cm:compliance-functional-id>d3879e8d5f</cm:compliance-functional-id>
<cm:compliance-info>This setting controls whether local administrators are allowed to create local firewall rules that apply together with firewall rules configured by Group Policy.

The recommended state for this setting is: No.

Note: When the Apply local firewall rules setting is configured to No, it&apos;s recommended to also configure the Display a notification setting to No. Otherwise, users will continue to receive messages that ask if they want to unblock a restricted inbound connection, but the user&apos;s response will be ignored.

Rationale:

When in the Public profile, there should be no special local firewall exceptions per computer. These settings should be managed by a centralized policy.

Impact:

Administrators can still create firewall rules, but the rules will not be applied.</cm:compliance-info>
<cm:compliance-result>FAILED</cm:compliance-result>
<cm:compliance-informational-id>a498f2b378c6d50ae4661d5349d569c36917f68145db7c97779a4c5a65c4d23d</cm:compliance-informational-id>
