<cm:compliance-check-name>2.3.10.1 Ensure &apos;Network access: Allow anonymous SID/Name translation&apos; is set to &apos;Disabled&apos;</cm:compliance-check-name>
<cm:compliance-check-id>2b1d3fd0fd02b75ac40d14ad5a57f7a1914444fd0c2b03180981cb2048e8f5df</cm:compliance-check-id>
<cm:compliance-actual-value>&apos;disabled&apos;</cm:compliance-actual-value>
<cm:compliance-source>custom</cm:compliance-source>
<cm:compliance-audit-file>CIS_MS_Windows_10_Enterprise_Level_1_v2.0.0.audit</cm:compliance-audit-file>
<cm:compliance-policy-value>&apos;disabled&apos;</cm:compliance-policy-value>
<cm:compliance-functional-id>03803f9df2</cm:compliance-functional-id>
<cm:compliance-info>This policy setting determines whether an anonymous user can request security identifier (SID) attributes for another user, or use a SID to obtain its corresponding user name.

The recommended state for this setting is: Disabled.

Rationale:

If this policy setting is enabled, a user with local access could use the well-known Administrator&apos;s SID to learn the real name of the built-in Administrator account, even if it has been renamed. That person could then use the account name to initiate a password guessing attack.

Impact:

None - this is the default behavior.</cm:compliance-info>
<cm:compliance-result>PASSED</cm:compliance-result>
<cm:compliance-informational-id>054135afa69bafb3c30f85899d4684af6825428d4d67b82278d726193745f980</cm:compliance-informational-id>
