<script_version>$Revision: 1.721 $</script_version>
<cm:compliance-benchmark-version>2.0.0</cm:compliance-benchmark-version>
<cm:compliance-check-name>5.6 Ensure access to the su command is restricted - wheel group contains root</cm:compliance-check-name>
<cm:compliance-check-id>e45c230c1fc7898e3f1953dd635daf30ba8b546a234a307c404ccc9852eb01ef</cm:compliance-check-id>
<cm:compliance-actual-value>The command &apos;grep ^wheel: /etc/group&apos; returned : 

wheel:x:10:auditor,student</cm:compliance-actual-value>
<cm:compliance-source>custom</cm:compliance-source>
<cm:compliance-audit-file>CIS_Distribution_Independent_Linux_Server_L1_v2.0.0.audit</cm:compliance-audit-file>
<cm:compliance-policy-value>cmd: grep ^wheel: /etc/group
expect: ^wheel:x:10:(?=.*root){1}.*$
system: Linux</cm:compliance-policy-value>
<cm:compliance-functional-id>a857d56636</cm:compliance-functional-id>
<cm:compliance-uname>Linux alma.aud507.local 4.18.0-372.32.1.el8_6.x86_64 #1 SMP Tue Oct 25 05:53:57 EDT 2022 x86_64 x86_64 x86_64 GNU/Linux</cm:compliance-uname>
<cm:compliance-info>The su command allows a user to run a command or shell as another user. The program
has been superseded by sudo , which allows for more granular control over privileged
access. Normally, the su command can be executed by any user. By uncommenting the
pam_wheel.so statement in /etc/pam.d/su , the su command will only allow users in the
wheel group to execute su .

Rationale:

Restricting the use of su , and using sudo in its place, provides system administrators better
control of the escalation of user privileges to execute privileged commands. The sudo utility
also provides a better logging and audit mechanism, as it can log each command executed
via sudo , whereas su can only record that a user executed the su program.</cm:compliance-info>
<cm:compliance-result>FAILED</cm:compliance-result>
<cm:compliance-informational-id>6ce6b901c95af8b4515e9fd2c9fccc13f55e9c0986f0cc384c552f48b921544c</cm:compliance-informational-id>
<cm:compliance-reference>800-171|3.4.2,800-53|CM-6,800-53r5|CM-6,CSCv6|5.1,CSCv7|5.1,CSF|PR.IP-1,GDPR|32.1.b,HIPAA|164.306(a)(1),ITSG-33|CM-6,LEVEL|1S,SWIFT-CSCv1|2.3</cm:compliance-reference>
<cm:compliance-solution>Add the following line to the /etc/pam.d/su file:

auth required pam_wheel.so use_uid

Create a comma separated list of users in the wheel statement in the /etc/group file:

wheel:x:10:root,&lt;user list&gt;</cm:compliance-solution>
<cm:compliance-benchmark-name>CIS Distribution Independent Linux Server L1</cm:compliance-benchmark-name>
<cm:compliance-control-id>9f88b30e80c0a01b17b9034dc7fee2836a8467ff1e1fb5705639ac477ae47ee8</cm:compliance-control-id>
<cm:compliance-see-also>https://workbench.cisecurity.org/files/2420</cm:compliance-see-also>
<cm:compliance-full-id>e45c230c1fc7898e3f1953dd635daf30ba8b546a234a307c404ccc9852eb01ef</cm:compliance-full-id>
</ReportItem>
