<cm:compliance-benchmark-version>2.0.0</cm:compliance-benchmark-version>
<cm:compliance-check-name>6.2.20 Ensure shadow group is empty</cm:compliance-check-name>
<cm:compliance-check-id>496cd99e46215980933451805a30b53a97d9dc279e7cca15b373b4450d442fed</cm:compliance-check-id>
<cm:compliance-actual-value>The command &apos;awk -F: &apos;FILENAME == &quot;/etc/group&quot; &amp;&amp; $1 == &quot;shadow&quot; { gid=$3; if ($4!=&quot;&quot;) { print &quot;secondary &quot;$4; f=1 } } FILENAME == &quot;/etc/passwd&quot; &amp;&amp; $4 == gid &amp;&amp; gid != &quot;&quot; { print &quot;primary &quot;$1; f=1; } END { if (!f) print &quot;shadow group empty&quot; }&apos; /etc/group /etc/passwd&apos; returned : 

shadow group empty</cm:compliance-actual-value>
<cm:compliance-source>custom</cm:compliance-source>
<cm:compliance-audit-file>CIS_Distribution_Independent_Linux_Server_L1_v2.0.0.audit</cm:compliance-audit-file>
<cm:compliance-policy-value>cmd: awk -F: &apos;FILENAME == &quot;/etc/group&quot; &amp;&amp; $1 == &quot;shadow&quot; { gid=$3; if ($4!=&quot;&quot;) { print &quot;secondary &quot;$4; f=1 } } FILENAME == &quot;/etc/passwd&quot; &amp;&amp; $4 == gid &amp;&amp; gid != &quot;&quot; { print &quot;primary &quot;$1; f=1; } END { if (!f) print &quot;shadow group empty&quot; }&apos; /etc/group /etc/passwd
expect: ^shadow group empty$
system: Linux</cm:compliance-policy-value>
<cm:compliance-functional-id>7579988db8</cm:compliance-functional-id>
<cm:compliance-uname>Linux alma.aud507.local 4.18.0-372.32.1.el8_6.x86_64 #1 SMP Tue Oct 25 05:53:57 EDT 2022 x86_64 x86_64 x86_64 GNU/Linux</cm:compliance-uname>
<cm:compliance-info>The shadow group allows system programs which require access the ability to read the
/etc/shadow file. No users should be assigned to the shadow group.

Rationale:

Any users assigned to the shadow group would be granted read access to the /etc/shadow
file. If attackers can gain read access to the /etc/shadow file, they can easily run a password
cracking program against the hashed passwords to break them. Other security information
that is stored in the /etc/shadow file (such as expiration) could also be useful to subvert
additional user accounts.</cm:compliance-info>
<cm:compliance-result>PASSED</cm:compliance-result>
<cm:compliance-informational-id>881f7abc73efc68a0f4ef72103dfee6e5ee8de116b3c649f01e6d2929f728b87</cm:compliance-informational-id>
<cm:compliance-reference>800-171|3.4.2,800-53|CM-6,800-53r5|CM-6,CSCv6|5.1,CSCv7|5.1,CSF|PR.IP-1,GDPR|32.1.b,HIPAA|164.306(a)(1),ITSG-33|CM-6,LEVEL|1S,SWIFT-CSCv1|2.3</cm:compliance-reference>
<cm:compliance-solution>Remove all users from the shadow group, and change the primary group of any users with
shadow as their primary group.</cm:compliance-solution>
<cm:compliance-benchmark-name>CIS Distribution Independent Linux Server L1</cm:compliance-benchmark-name>
<cm:compliance-control-id>b7f3857821b755f1c2345bacaa04b86db4b2d4b46e951b751fd55ed62df142ff</cm:compliance-control-id>
<cm:compliance-see-also>https://workbench.cisecurity.org/files/2420</cm:compliance-see-also>
<cm:compliance-full-id>496cd99e46215980933451805a30b53a97d9dc279e7cca15b373b4450d442fed</cm:compliance-full-id>
</ReportItem>
