<cm:compliance-actual-value>The command &apos;cat /etc/passwd | egrep -v &apos;^(root|halt|sync|shutdown)&apos; | awk -F: &apos;($7 !~ &quot;(/usr)?/sbin/nologin&quot; &amp;&amp; $7 != &quot;/bin/false&quot;) { print $1 &quot; &quot; $6 }&apos; | while read user dir; do if [ ! -d &quot;$dir&quot; ]; then echo &quot;The home directory ($dir) of user $user does not exist.&quot;; else if [ ! -h &quot;$dir/.netrc&quot; -a -f &quot;$dir/.netrc&quot; ]; then echo &quot;.netrc file $dir/.netrc exists&quot;; fi; fi; done | awk &apos;{ print } END { if (NR==0) print &quot;No users have netrc files&quot; }&apos;&apos; returned : 

No users have netrc files</cm:compliance-actual-value>
<cm:compliance-source>custom</cm:compliance-source>
<cm:compliance-audit-file>CIS_Distribution_Independent_Linux_Server_L1_v2.0.0.audit</cm:compliance-audit-file>
<cm:compliance-policy-value>cmd: cat /etc/passwd | egrep -v &apos;^(root|halt|sync|shutdown)&apos; | awk -F: &apos;($7 !~ &quot;(/usr)?/sbin/nologin&quot; &amp;&amp; $7 != &quot;/bin/false&quot;) { print $1 &quot; &quot; $6 }&apos; | while read user dir; do if [ ! -d &quot;$dir&quot; ]; then echo &quot;The home directory ($dir) of user $user does not exist.&quot;; else if [ ! -h &quot;$dir/.netrc&quot; -a -f &quot;$dir/.netrc&quot; ]; then echo &quot;.netrc file $dir/.netrc exists&quot;; fi; fi; done | awk &apos;{ print } END { if (NR==0) print &quot;No users have netrc files&quot; }&apos;
expect: No users have netrc files
system: Linux</cm:compliance-policy-value>
<cm:compliance-functional-id>fb0229f22f</cm:compliance-functional-id>
<cm:compliance-uname>Linux alma.aud507.local 4.18.0-372.32.1.el8_6.x86_64 #1 SMP Tue Oct 25 05:53:57 EDT 2022 x86_64 x86_64 x86_64 GNU/Linux</cm:compliance-uname>
<cm:compliance-info>The .netrc file contains data for logging into a remote host for file transfers via FTP.

Rationale:

The .netrc file presents a significant security risk since it stores passwords in unencrypted
form. Even if FTP is disabled, user accounts may have brought over .netrc files from other
systems which could pose a risk to those systems.</cm:compliance-info>
<cm:compliance-result>PASSED</cm:compliance-result>
<cm:compliance-informational-id>59629d459854104881ca541d1c1cdc950b76c7bdeced5ba71ce421075e555a08</cm:compliance-informational-id>
<cm:compliance-reference>800-171|3.5.2,800-53|IA-5,800-53|IA-5(1),800-53r5|IA-5,800-53r5|IA-5(1),CSCv6|16.14,CSCv7|16.4,CSF|PR.AC-1,GDPR|32.1.b,HIPAA|164.306(a)(1),HIPAA|164.312(a)(2)(i),HIPAA|164.312(d),ITSG-33|IA-5,ITSG-33|IA-5(1),LEVEL|1S,NESA|T5.2.3,QCSC-v1|5.2.2,QCSC-v1|13.2,SWIFT-CSCv1|4.1</cm:compliance-reference>
<cm:compliance-solution>Making global modifications to users&apos; files without alerting the user community can result
in unexpected outages and unhappy users. Therefore, it is recommended that a monitoring
policy be established to report user .netrc files and determine the action to be taken in
accordance with site policy.

Notes:

On some distributions the /sbin/nologin should be replaced with /usr/sbin/nologin.</cm:compliance-solution>
<cm:compliance-benchmark-name>CIS Distribution Independent Linux Server L1</cm:compliance-benchmark-name>
<cm:compliance-control-id>c91e4a6e3581d1c1fd78cc1430dc89b984126806bd91344fbb69abbf51283911</cm:compliance-control-id>
