<cm:compliance-benchmark-version>2.0.0</cm:compliance-benchmark-version>
<cm:compliance-check-name>6.1.9 Ensure permissions on /etc/gshadow- are configured</cm:compliance-check-name>
<cm:compliance-check-id>1a8077822688b10efe037a5891b6f23f708c13d51368e5d4536615dc32ac8c76</cm:compliance-check-id>
<cm:compliance-actual-value>The file /etc/gshadow- with fmode owner: root group: root mode: 0000 uid: 0 gid: 0 uneven permissions : FALSE is compliant with the policy value 

/etc/gshadow-</cm:compliance-actual-value>
<cm:compliance-source>custom</cm:compliance-source>
<cm:compliance-audit-file>CIS_Distribution_Independent_Linux_Server_L1_v2.0.0.audit</cm:compliance-audit-file>
<cm:compliance-policy-value>file: /etc/gshadow-
group: root
mask: 137
owner: root
system: Linux</cm:compliance-policy-value>
<cm:compliance-functional-id>b5f861fd77</cm:compliance-functional-id>
<cm:compliance-uname>Linux alma.aud507.local 4.18.0-372.32.1.el8_6.x86_64 #1 SMP Tue Oct 25 05:53:57 EDT 2022 x86_64 x86_64 x86_64 GNU/Linux</cm:compliance-uname>
<cm:compliance-info>The /etc/gshadow- file is used to store backup information about groups that is critical to
the security of those accounts, such as the hashed password and other security
information.

Rationale:

It is critical to ensure that the /etc/gshadow- file is protected from unauthorized access.
Although it is protected by default, the file permissions could be changed either
inadvertently or through malicious actions.</cm:compliance-info>
<cm:compliance-result>PASSED</cm:compliance-result>
<cm:compliance-informational-id>718c48c33df4502f3e26fe8947b6e99cddea8a13cc4729566f39262cec270a53</cm:compliance-informational-id>
<cm:compliance-reference>800-171|3.5.2,800-53|IA-5,800-53|IA-5(1),800-53r5|IA-5,800-53r5|IA-5(1),CSCv6|16.14,CSCv7|16.4,CSF|PR.AC-1,GDPR|32.1.b,HIPAA|164.306(a)(1),HIPAA|164.312(a)(2)(i),HIPAA|164.312(d),ITSG-33|IA-5,ITSG-33|IA-5(1),LEVEL|1S,NESA|T5.2.3,QCSC-v1|5.2.2,QCSC-v1|13.2,SWIFT-CSCv1|4.1</cm:compliance-reference>
<cm:compliance-solution>Run the one of the following chown commands as appropriate and the chmod to set
permissions on /etc/gshadow- :

# chown root:root /etc/gshadow-
# chown root:shadow /etc/gshadow-
# chmod o-rwx,g-rw /etc/gshadow-</cm:compliance-solution>
<cm:compliance-benchmark-name>CIS Distribution Independent Linux Server L1</cm:compliance-benchmark-name>
<cm:compliance-control-id>44211cc423e721a33e4596a0144bb5d8b1a2f7f6572bce29fc14bb39e5720aec</cm:compliance-control-id>
<cm:compliance-see-also>https://workbench.cisecurity.org/files/2420</cm:compliance-see-also>
<cm:compliance-full-id>1a8077822688b10efe037a5891b6f23f708c13d51368e5d4536615dc32ac8c76</cm:compliance-full-id>
</ReportItem>
