<cm:compliance-actual-value>The file /etc/group with fmode owner: root group: root mode: 0644 uid: 0 gid: 0 uneven permissions : FALSE is compliant with the policy value 

/etc/group</cm:compliance-actual-value>
<cm:compliance-source>custom</cm:compliance-source>
<cm:compliance-audit-file>CIS_Distribution_Independent_Linux_Server_L1_v2.0.0.audit</cm:compliance-audit-file>
<cm:compliance-policy-value>file: /etc/group
group: root
mask: 133
owner: root
system: Linux</cm:compliance-policy-value>
<cm:compliance-functional-id>44924b2412</cm:compliance-functional-id>
<cm:compliance-uname>Linux alma.aud507.local 4.18.0-372.32.1.el8_6.x86_64 #1 SMP Tue Oct 25 05:53:57 EDT 2022 x86_64 x86_64 x86_64 GNU/Linux</cm:compliance-uname>
<cm:compliance-info>The /etc/group file contains a list of all the valid groups defined in the system. The
command below allows read/write access for root and read access for everyone else.

Rationale:

The /etc/group file needs to be protected from unauthorized changes by non-privileged
users, but needs to be readable as this information is used with many non-privileged
programs.</cm:compliance-info>
<cm:compliance-result>PASSED</cm:compliance-result>
<cm:compliance-informational-id>316fcc0819d27147cb8537ef2560d64212bbf52ddd812c4d433c2a0409071044</cm:compliance-informational-id>
