<cm:compliance-actual-value>The command returned : 

grep: /etc/securetty: No such file or directory
none</cm:compliance-actual-value>
<cm:compliance-source>custom</cm:compliance-source>
<cm:compliance-audit-file>CIS_Distribution_Independent_Linux_Server_L1_v2.0.0.audit</cm:compliance-audit-file>
<cm:compliance-policy-value>cmd: g*******
dont_echo_cmd: YES
expect: none
system: Linux</cm:compliance-policy-value>
<cm:compliance-functional-id>87fec7d0b7</cm:compliance-functional-id>
<cm:compliance-uname>Linux alma.aud507.local 4.18.0-372.32.1.el8_6.x86_64 #1 SMP Tue Oct 25 05:53:57 EDT 2022 x86_64 x86_64 x86_64 GNU/Linux</cm:compliance-uname>
<cm:compliance-info>The file /etc/securetty contains a list of valid terminals that may be logged in directly as
root.

Rationale:

Since the system console has special properties to handle emergency situations, it is
important to ensure that the console is in a physically secure location and that
unauthorized consoles have not been defined.</cm:compliance-info>
<cm:compliance-result>PASSED</cm:compliance-result>
<cm:compliance-informational-id>0f117558702d04b802b2a9b327943cd6dcc2ea6a0f4cfa4695ff0769e35e349a</cm:compliance-informational-id>
