<cm:compliance-policy-value>file: /etc/ssh/ssh_host_*_key.pub
group: root
mask: 7133
owner: root
system: Linux</cm:compliance-policy-value>
<cm:compliance-functional-id>c7bcac4c31</cm:compliance-functional-id>
<cm:compliance-uname>Linux alma.aud507.local 4.18.0-372.32.1.el8_6.x86_64 #1 SMP Tue Oct 25 05:53:57 EDT 2022 x86_64 x86_64 x86_64 GNU/Linux</cm:compliance-uname>
<cm:compliance-info>An SSH public key is one of two files used in SSH public key authentication. In this
authentication method, a public key is a key that can be used for verifying digital signatures
generated using a corresponding private key. Only a public key that corresponds to a
private key will be able to authenticate successfully.

Rationale:

If a public host key file is modified by an unauthorized user, the SSH service may be
compromised.</cm:compliance-info>
<cm:compliance-result>PASSED</cm:compliance-result>
<cm:compliance-informational-id>1c5ca37856913f1821540d3b301265fa9689f1238603c872522fa56ba98fc557</cm:compliance-informational-id>
<cm:compliance-reference>800-171|3.4.2,800-53|CM-6,800-53r5|CM-6,CSCv6|5.1,CSCv7|5.1,CSF|PR.IP-1,GDPR|32.1.b,HIPAA|164.306(a)(1),ITSG-33|CM-6,LEVEL|1S,SWIFT-CSCv1|2.3</cm:compliance-reference>
<cm:compliance-solution>Run the following commands to set permissions and ownership on the SSH host public key
files

# find /etc/ssh -xdev -type f -name &apos;ssh_host_*_key.pub&apos; -exec chmod 0644 {} \;

#find /etc/ssh -xdev -type f -name &apos;ssh_host_*_key.pub&apos; -exec chown root:root {} \;</cm:compliance-solution>
<cm:compliance-benchmark-name>CIS Distribution Independent Linux Server L1</cm:compliance-benchmark-name>
