<cm:compliance-benchmark-version>2.0.0</cm:compliance-benchmark-version>
<cm:compliance-check-name>1.1.16 Ensure nosuid option set on /dev/shm partition</cm:compliance-check-name>
<cm:compliance-check-id>5cab787dee4c28171866df3e2fee8834a547f792f6df2e2b3bcb878d576dc9d2</cm:compliance-check-id>
<cm:compliance-actual-value>The command &apos;mount | grep &apos;on /dev/shm &apos;&apos; returned : 

tmpfs on /dev/shm type tmpfs (rw,nosuid,nodev,seclabel)</cm:compliance-actual-value>
<cm:compliance-source>custom</cm:compliance-source>
<cm:compliance-audit-file>CIS_Distribution_Independent_Linux_Server_L1_v2.0.0.audit</cm:compliance-audit-file>
<cm:compliance-policy-value>cmd: mount | grep &apos;on /dev/shm &apos;
expect: nosuid
system: Linux</cm:compliance-policy-value>
<cm:compliance-functional-id>9aa4d55367</cm:compliance-functional-id>
<cm:compliance-uname>Linux alma.aud507.local 4.18.0-372.32.1.el8_6.x86_64 #1 SMP Tue Oct 25 05:53:57 EDT 2022 x86_64 x86_64 x86_64 GNU/Linux</cm:compliance-uname>
<cm:compliance-info>The nosuid mount option specifies that the filesystem cannot contain setuid files.

Rationale:

Setting this option on a file system prevents users from introducing privileged programs
onto the system and allowing non-root users to execute them.</cm:compliance-info>
<cm:compliance-result>PASSED</cm:compliance-result>
<cm:compliance-informational-id>e124e5166e41842de29649e6f96211f1c07f3aa945365991e01154efad294fa1</cm:compliance-informational-id>
<cm:compliance-reference>800-171|3.4.2,800-53|CM-6,800-53r5|CM-6,CSCv6|13,CSCv7|5.1,CSF|PR.IP-1,GDPR|32.1.b,HIPAA|164.306(a)(1),ITSG-33|CM-6,LEVEL|1S,SWIFT-CSCv1|2.3</cm:compliance-reference>
<cm:compliance-solution>Edit the /etc/fstab file and add nosuid to the fourth field (mounting options) for the
/dev/shm partition. See the fstab(5) manual page for more information.
Run the following command to remount /dev/shm :

# mount -o remount,nosuid /dev/shm

Notes:

Some distributions mount /dev/shm through other means and require /dev/shm to be
added to /etc/fstab even though it is already being mounted on boot. Others may
configure /dev/shm in other locations and may override /etc/fstab configuration. Consult
the documentation appropriate for your distribution.</cm:compliance-solution>
<cm:compliance-benchmark-name>CIS Distribution Independent Linux Server L1</cm:compliance-benchmark-name>
<cm:compliance-control-id>167879a028518bf484c7e6060761060699989ac55c2beae2b99dcca65a044409</cm:compliance-control-id>
<cm:compliance-see-also>https://workbench.cisecurity.org/files/2420</cm:compliance-see-also>
<cm:compliance-full-id>5cab787dee4c28171866df3e2fee8834a547f792f6df2e2b3bcb878d576dc9d2</cm:compliance-full-id>
</ReportItem>
