<cm:compliance-functional-id>9073f0d2c1</cm:compliance-functional-id>
<cm:compliance-uname>Linux ubuntu 5.15.0-84-generic #93-Ubuntu SMP Tue Sep 5 17:16:10 UTC 2023 x86_64 x86_64 x86_64 GNU/Linux</cm:compliance-uname>
<cm:compliance-info>User accounts that have been inactive for over a given period of time can be automatically disabled. It is recommended that accounts that are inactive for 30 days after password expiration be disabled.

Rationale:

Inactive accounts pose a threat to system security since the users are not logging in to notice failed login attempts or other anomalies.</cm:compliance-info>
<cm:compliance-result>FAILED</cm:compliance-result>
<cm:compliance-informational-id>e39bdc5f6f09c074bbc1135374aa8340c0b4888baad40847244ffeabd6fcdf7b</cm:compliance-informational-id>
<cm:compliance-reference>800-171|3.5.2,800-53|IA-5(1),800-53r5|IA-5(1),CSCv7|4.4,CSCv8|5.2,CSF|PR.AC-1,GDPR|32.1.b,HIPAA|164.306(a)(1),HIPAA|164.312(a)(2)(i),HIPAA|164.312(d),ITSG-33|IA-5(1),LEVEL|1A,NESA|T5.2.3,QCSC-v1|5.2.2,QCSC-v1|13.2,SWIFT-CSCv1|4.1</cm:compliance-reference>
<cm:compliance-solution>Run the following command to set the default password inactivity period to 30 days:

# useradd -D -f 30

Modify user parameters for all users with a password set to match:

# chage --inactive 30 &lt;user&gt;

Default Value:

INACTIVE=-1

Additional Information:

A value of -1 would disable this setting</cm:compliance-solution>
<cm:compliance-benchmark-name>CIS Ubuntu Linux 22.04 LTS Server L1</cm:compliance-benchmark-name>
