<cm:compliance-check-name>5.2.18 Ensure SSH MaxAuthTries is set to 4 or less</cm:compliance-check-name>
<cm:compliance-check-id>76264bf9a7d4c783c44a961cbfbc4dcdbfcad1ab10a272928badcbded375aeb8</cm:compliance-check-id>
<cm:compliance-actual-value>The file &quot;/etc/ssh/sshd_config&quot; does not contain &quot;^[\s]*[Mm]ax[Aa]uth[Tt]ries[\s]&quot;</cm:compliance-actual-value>
<cm:compliance-source>custom</cm:compliance-source>
<cm:compliance-audit-file>CIS_Ubuntu_22.04_LTS_v1.0.0_Server_L1.audit</cm:compliance-audit-file>
<cm:compliance-policy-value>expect: ^[\s]*[Mm]ax[Aa]uth[Tt]ries[\s]+[1-4][\s]*$
file: /etc/ssh/sshd_config
regex: ^[\s]*[Mm]ax[Aa]uth[Tt]ries[\s]
system: Linux</cm:compliance-policy-value>
<cm:compliance-functional-id>9bbb0a6623</cm:compliance-functional-id>
<cm:compliance-uname>Linux ubuntu 5.15.0-84-generic #93-Ubuntu SMP Tue Sep 5 17:16:10 UTC 2023 x86_64 x86_64 x86_64 GNU/Linux</cm:compliance-uname>
<cm:compliance-info>The MaxAuthTries parameter specifies the maximum number of authentication attempts permitted per connection. When the login failure count reaches half the number, error messages will be written to the syslog file detailing the login failure.

Rationale:

Setting the MaxAuthTries parameter to a low number will minimize the risk of successful brute force attacks to the SSH server. While the recommended setting is 4, set the number based on site policy.</cm:compliance-info>
<cm:compliance-result>FAILED</cm:compliance-result>
