<cm:compliance-check-name>5.4.5 Ensure all current passwords uses the configured hashing algorithm</cm:compliance-check-name>
<cm:compliance-check-id>857b6d9edbdba92bc132f30b7179c27259aac977549936686da7a40c2eddeed7</cm:compliance-check-id>
<cm:compliance-source>custom</cm:compliance-source>
<cm:compliance-audit-file>CIS_Ubuntu_22.04_LTS_v1.0.0_Server_L1.audit</cm:compliance-audit-file>
<cm:compliance-functional-id>fbcf1532c1</cm:compliance-functional-id>
<cm:compliance-uname>Linux ubuntu 5.15.0-84-generic #93-Ubuntu SMP Tue Sep 5 17:16:10 UTC 2023 x86_64 x86_64 x86_64 GNU/Linux</cm:compliance-uname>
<cm:compliance-info>Currently used passwords with out of date hashing algorithms may pose a security risk to the system.

Rationale:

In use passwords should always match the configured hashing algorithm for the system.

Impact:

If the administrator forces a password change, this could cause a large spike in CPU usage if a large number of users change their password during the same time.

NOTE: Nessus has provided the target output to assist in reviewing the benchmark to ensure target compliance.</cm:compliance-info>
<cm:compliance-result>ERROR</cm:compliance-result>
<cm:compliance-informational-id>74f25e4b5bb7f3c8db85849a54ef7e9e8e2e83dec2dbf8de23399e543235314d</cm:compliance-informational-id>
<cm:compliance-reference>800-171|3.5.2,800-171|3.13.16,800-53|IA-5(1),800-53|SC-28,800-53|SC-28(1),800-53r5|IA-5(1),800-53r5|SC-28,800-53r5|SC-28(1),CN-L3|8.1.4.7(b),CN-L3|8.1.4.8(b),CSCv7|16.4,CSCv8|3.11,CSF|PR.AC-1,CSF|PR.DS-1,GDPR|32.1.a,GDPR|32.1.b,HIPAA|164.306(a)(1),HIPAA|164.312(a)(2)(i),HIPAA|164.312(a)(2)(iv),HIPAA|164.312(d),HIPAA|164.312(e)(2)(ii),ITSG-33|IA-5(1),ITSG-33|SC-28,ITSG-33|SC-28(1),ITSG-33|SC-28a.,LEVEL|1M,NESA|T5.2.3,PCI-DSSv3.2.1|3.4,PCI-DSSv4.0|3.3.2,PCI-DSSv4.0|3.5.1,QCSC-v1|5.2.2,QCSC-v1|6.2,QCSC-v1|13.2,SWIFT-CSCv1|4.1,TBA-FIISB|28.1</cm:compliance-reference>
<cm:compliance-solution>If the administrator wish to force an immediate change on all users as per the output of the audit, execute:

#!/usr/bin/env bash

{
   UID_MIN=$(awk &apos;/^s*UID_MIN/{print $2}&apos; /etc/login.defs)
   awk -F: -v UID_MIN=&apos;${UID_MIN}&apos; &apos;( $3 &gt;= UID_MIN &amp;&amp; $1 != &apos;nfsnobody&apos; ) { print $1 }&apos; /etc/passwd | xargs -n 1 chage -d 0
}

NOTE: This could cause significant temporary CPU load on the system if a large number of users reset their passwords at the same time.</cm:compliance-solution>
<cm:compliance-benchmark-name>CIS Ubuntu Linux 22.04 LTS Server L1</cm:compliance-benchmark-name>
<cm:compliance-control-id>847db33fb78abf2f9cced351ffac6f4a088e54e84075b462d740042b01c16dcd</cm:compliance-control-id>
<cm:compliance-see-also>https://workbench.cisecurity.org/files/4068</cm:compliance-see-also>
<cm:compliance-full-id>857b6d9edbdba92bc132f30b7179c27259aac977549936686da7a40c2eddeed7</cm:compliance-full-id>
