<cm:compliance-check-name>5.3.1 Ensure sudo is installed</cm:compliance-check-name>
<cm:compliance-check-id>fe8621a2ba15fea3352a50cf3f6bfc001e4a6620cee4ef5379faed5e40518084</cm:compliance-check-id>
<cm:compliance-source>custom</cm:compliance-source>
<cm:compliance-audit-file>CIS_Ubuntu_22.04_LTS_v1.0.0_Server_L1.audit</cm:compliance-audit-file>
<cm:compliance-functional-id>d1f83110d2</cm:compliance-functional-id>
<cm:compliance-uname>Linux ubuntu 5.15.0-84-generic #93-Ubuntu SMP Tue Sep 5 17:16:10 UTC 2023 x86_64 x86_64 x86_64 GNU/Linux</cm:compliance-uname>
<cm:compliance-info>sudo allows a permitted user to execute a command as the superuser or another user, as specified by the security policy. The invoking user&apos;s real (not effective) user ID is used to determine the user name with which to query the security policy.

Rationale:

sudo supports a plug-in architecture for security policies and input/output logging. Third parties can develop and distribute their own policy and I/O logging plug-ins to work seamlessly with the sudo front end. The default security policy is sudoers, which is configured via the file /etc/sudoers and any entries in /etc/sudoers.d.

The security policy determines what privileges, if any, a user has to run sudo. The policy may require that users authenticate themselves with a password or another authentication mechanism. If authentication is required, sudo will exit if the user&apos;s password is not entered within a configurable time limit. This limit is policy-specific.</cm:compliance-info>
<cm:compliance-result>ERROR</cm:compliance-result>
<cm:compliance-informational-id>0d3d7f821c60515e33764bb7c3e31aa5628c9ebf4703bff96436890dfee721cf</cm:compliance-informational-id>
<cm:compliance-reference>800-171|3.1.5,800-171|3.1.6,800-53|AC-6(2),800-53|AC-6(5),800-53r5|AC-6(2),800-53r5|AC-6(5),CN-L3|7.1.3.2(b),CN-L3|7.1.3.2(g),CN-L3|8.1.4.2(d),CN-L3|8.1.10.6(a),CSCv7|4.3,CSCv8|5.4,CSF|PR.AC-4,GDPR|32.1.b,HIPAA|164.306(a)(1),HIPAA|164.312(a)(1),ISO/IEC-27001|A.9.2.3,ITSG-33|AC-6(2),ITSG-33|AC-6(5),LEVEL|1A,NESA|T5.1.1,NESA|T5.2.2,NESA|T5.6.1,NIAv2|AM1,NIAv2|AM23f,NIAv2|AM32,NIAv2|AM33,NIAv2|SS13c,NIAv2|SS15c,NIAv2|VL3a,PCI-DSSv3.2.1|7.1.2,PCI-DSSv4.0|7.2.1,PCI-DSSv4.0|7.2.2,QCSC-v1|5.2.2,QCSC-v1|6.2,SWIFT-CSCv1|1.2,SWIFT-CSCv1|5.1,TBA-FIISB|31.4.2,TBA-FIISB|31.4.3</cm:compliance-reference>
<cm:compliance-solution>First determine is LDAP functionality is required. If so, then install sudo-ldap, else install sudo.
Example:

# apt install sudo</cm:compliance-solution>
